## Proof

**Answer: Yes.**

Since $\|u\|_{W^{k,p}(K)} \leq c$ for every relatively compact $K \subset U$, in particular $u \in W^{k,p}(K)$ for every such $K$, so $u \in W^{k,p}_{\mathrm{loc}}(U)$. Hence the weak derivatives $D^\alpha u$ exist as locally integrable functions on $U$ for all $|\alpha| \leq k$.

Take an exhaustion of $U$: a sequence of relatively compact open sets $U_1 \subset \subset U_2 \subset \subset \cdots$ with $\bigcup_{j=1}^{\infty} U_j = U$ (for instance, $U_j = \{x \in U : \mathrm{dist}(x, \partial U) > 1/j,\ |x| < j\}$ for $j$ sufficiently large).

**Case $1 \leq p < \infty$.** For each $|\alpha| \leq k$ and each $j$,
$$\|D^\alpha u\|_{L^p(U_j)}^p \leq \|u\|_{W^{k,p}(U_j)}^p \leq c^p.$$
Since $|D^\alpha u|^p \geq 0$ and $U_j \nearrow U$, the monotone convergence theorem gives
$$\int_U |D^\alpha u|^p\,dx = \lim_{j \to \infty} \int_{U_j} |D^\alpha u|^p\,dx \leq c^p < \infty.$$
Thus $D^\alpha u \in L^p(U)$ for every $|\alpha| \leq k$, so $u \in W^{k,p}(U)$.

**Case $p = \infty$.** For each $|\alpha| \leq k$ and each $j$,
$$\|D^\alpha u\|_{L^\infty(U_j)} \leq c.$$
Since $U_j \nearrow U$,
$$\|D^\alpha u\|_{L^\infty(U)} = \sup_{x \in U} |D^\alpha u(x)| = \lim_{j \to \infty} \|D^\alpha u\|_{L^\infty(U_j)} \leq c.$$
Thus $D^\alpha u \in L^\infty(U)$ for every $|\alpha| \leq k$, so $u \in W^{k,\infty}(U)$.

**Remark.** The uniformity of the bound in $K$ is essential. Without it, the hypothesis reduces to $u \in W^{k,p}_{\mathrm{loc}}(U)$, which does not imply $u \in W^{k,p}(U)$ (e.g. $u \equiv 1$ on $U = \mathbb{R}$, $p < \infty$). The constant $c$ independent of $K$ is precisely what forces the global $L^p$-norm to be finite via monotone convergence.

